# Tests skipped on the macOS (Darwin) fast-test run.
# Lines starting with '#' are comments; blank lines are ignored (see ci/jobs/fast_test.py).
# These are env/harness/portability issues that likely pass on the CI runner or are fixable,
# not permanent platform gaps - each is a candidate to re-verify or fix, not to keep forever.

# Times out on the macOS CI runner: the Python script hangs silently before sending any query
# (no query reaches the server), while the identical test with the same numpy/scipy/pandas versions
# passes in ~3s locally. Looks like a runner-specific hang in the scipy import. Needs further investigation.
02294_anova_cmp

# The local prefetched read pool requires the `pread_threadpool` read method, which needs
# `preadv2` with `RWF_NOWAIT` to check the page cache. The system call does not exist on
# macOS, so the default read method is switched to `pread` there and the pool never runs.
# These tests assert side effects of the pool (a failpoint inside it / asynchronous_read_counters
# in query_log), so they cannot pass on Darwin. This one is a platform gap, not a harness issue.
02910_prefetch_unexpceted_exception
04640_query_log_map_columns_serialization

# Marginal timeout on the macOS CI runner: the combined diagnostics test averaged ~47s there
# with a 60s per-test limit (vs ~27s on the Linux fast test), so a few percent of runs crossed
# the limit. The expensive driver-replay half was split into this test; the cheap
# marker/payload assertions stay in 03008_deduplication_diagnostics_opt_in, which keeps
# running here. Full coverage remains on Linux; re-enable if the runner gets faster.
03008_deduplication_diagnostics_replay

# Flaky on CI (stuck timeout)
04319_skip_unavailable_shards_mode_table_missing
